WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.620
Peace be upon you and welcome to the third meeting of our fellowship. In today's episode, we want to make a qualitative and important leap to build a real and living bridge connecting abstract medical facts with the daily reality of the community.

00:15.620 --> 00:25.520
The objective is to move from merely narrating dry information to building effective communication that generates a tangible impact in our community.

00:25.520 --> 00:40.360
In the previous video, we discussed and saw how health education is behavioral engineering and bridge building. Today, we want to dive into the core nerve of this bridge: how to understand the recipient and precisely comprehend community needs.

00:40.360 --> 00:49.440
Let us begin with this logical test: Imagine a massive loudspeaker in the middle of the street shouting: "Beware of high temperatures!"

00:49.440 --> 00:55.520
And on that same street, there is a child running after a football, a pregnant mother, and an elderly man walking slowly,

00:55.520 --> 00:58.400
leaning on a cane, suffering from kidney and cardiovascular problems.

00:58.400 --> 01:03.060
Do you expect with certainty that this unified message will change the behavior of anyone?

01:03.060 --> 01:06.400
Impossible. It will pass as ordinary words.

01:06.400 --> 01:09.340
The environmental danger is one: the heatwave.

01:09.340 --> 01:16.080
Yet the child's response differs 180 degrees from the young person's response, and differs from the elderly person's response.

01:16.080 --> 01:18.940
The priorities and concerns are completely different.

01:18.940 --> 01:23.240
From this point, we shatter the myth of the one-size-fits-all message.

01:23.240 --> 01:25.520
For this reason, we need to redefine

01:25.520 --> 01:30.920
the word "community." The subject is no longer mere geographical map lines or

01:30.920 --> 01:35.600
residential neighborhoods; rather, it is a human bond united by the same characteristics or

01:35.600 --> 01:41.060
shared challenges. Think of construction workers under the blazing midday sun

01:41.060 --> 01:45.620
burning on the asphalt; or mothers in primary healthcare

01:45.620 --> 01:50.720
clinics; or families in refugee and displacement camps; or the segment of

01:50.720 --> 01:55.520
youth and students. Each community among these has its own specific communication code,

01:55.520 --> 02:00.740
and information sources it trusts above others. The elderly person might trust the mosque imam

02:00.740 --> 02:05.180
or family doctor, whereas the youth rely on their convictions from

02:05.180 --> 02:10.100
digital platforms. This takes us to the second section: The triangle of behavioral

02:10.100 --> 02:14.600
determinants, or the trap we fall into when we think knowledge alone

02:14.600 --> 02:20.000
is sufficient to change behavior. If we deconstruct this triangle, we find that

02:20.000 --> 02:20.700
knowledge is

02:20.700 --> 02:25.200
merely the first step. Comprehending knowledge: Does the person in front of us

02:25.200 --> 02:30.120
perceive the correct action and understand it in simple language? And community culture and habits:

02:30.120 --> 02:35.100
A young worker at a construction site might refuse to take a rest break in the shade despite

02:35.100 --> 02:39.660
knowing the danger, believing that this shows weakness or a lack of

02:39.660 --> 02:44.220
masculinity. A single step governed by a belief like this is enough to disrupt all our efforts.

02:44.220 --> 02:49.440
And the environment and resource availability is the most dangerous side: Does it make sense to advise a family

02:49.440 --> 02:53.640
in a camp or marginalized neighborhood about the necessity of drinking pure, cooled water at all times,

02:53.640 --> 02:58.800
when their environment fundamentally lacks the simplest basics of clean drinking water? Knowledge

02:58.800 --> 03:03.420
without an environment or resources to support it means nothing. Now, how do we translate this

03:03.420 --> 03:08.640
practically through tailoring heatwave messages? With children, we will certainly not talk

03:08.640 --> 03:13.020
to them about thermal regulation or osmotic balance; rather, we enter

03:13.020 --> 03:17.340
into their world: "Heroes, drink water regularly while you play so you

03:17.340 --> 03:21.660
maintain your energy and strength like champions and do not get tired quickly." With

03:21.660 --> 03:25.320
the elderly, we are dealing with a biological reality where the brain's thirst

03:25.320 --> 03:29.520
sensation center weakens; therefore, the message must change to become: "Father,

03:29.520 --> 03:33.660
keep water near you always even if you do not feel thirsty, and pay attention

03:33.660 --> 03:38.160
to any slight dizziness or heaviness in the head, and ask for help immediately." And with pregnant

03:38.160 --> 03:42.180
women, you are addressing maternal instinct and double physiological

03:42.180 --> 03:46.920
effort: "Your body produces extra heat for your baby's growth; your

03:46.920 --> 03:50.820
continuous drinking of water and avoiding standing before cooking ovens or going out at

03:50.820 --> 03:56.100
midday protects you and ensures the safe flow of nourishment to your fetus." The scientific

03:56.100 --> 04:01.440
content did not change, but we crafted the mold intelligently to suit the recipient. This

04:01.440 --> 04:06.600
leads us to the fourth section: Climate vulnerability and health equity. Climate

04:06.600 --> 04:10.440
never distributes risks equitably: Children lose fluids

04:10.440 --> 04:11.980
rapidly; the elderly

04:12.180 --> 04:16.500
experience declining heart efficiency and heat regulation; pregnant women carry a sensitive

04:16.500 --> 04:21.360
physiological burden vulnerable to dehydration; field laborers and tent dwellers lack

04:21.360 --> 04:25.020
thermal insulation and air conditioning. These are the ones who pay the highest price.

04:25.020 --> 04:29.700
And here comes the strategic role of the health educator: They do not distribute effort

04:29.700 --> 04:33.840
in naive mathematical equality; rather, they practice equity and direct 80

04:33.840 --> 04:37.140
percent of their energy and field resources to protect these most vulnerable

04:37.140 --> 04:42.240
and marginalized groups first. Finally, we reach the most important tool: Active

04:42.240 --> 04:46.260
community listening skills, because successful health education is reciprocal

04:46.260 --> 04:51.360
dialogue, not a top-down lecture. The health educator does not enter as an arrogant expert

04:51.360 --> 04:56.520
giving orders; rather, they sit with families and drink a cup of tea with them, building dialogue

04:56.520 --> 05:01.560
that generates trust through golden questions: "What genuinely worries and frightens you

05:01.560 --> 05:05.880
this summer?" to remove barriers and uncover priorities. "What are the real difficulties

05:05.880 --> 05:09.660
preventing you from drinking water or taking rest periods?"

05:09.660 --> 05:14.220
This is to search for material and cultural obstacles instead of placing blame

05:14.220 --> 05:19.380
on them. "How can we work together to make these recommendations applicable

05:19.380 --> 05:23.940
in your neighborhood?" This is to entrench partnership and find solutions springing from

05:23.940 --> 05:29.040
their own capabilities. Memorize this compass well: Do not start your day saying:

05:29.040 --> 05:33.720
"What will I tell the people?" Start with the question: "Who are these people? And what do they

05:33.720 --> 05:38.040
genuinely need to hear to save their lives?" Now, after understanding

05:38.040 --> 05:42.660
our audience, deconstructing their behavioral determinants, and identifying vulnerable groups, we are ready

05:42.660 --> 05:46.740
for the next major step in the fourth video: How to design the educational message

05:46.740 --> 05:51.600
professionally, and select effective molds and mediums to guarantee

05:51.600 --> 05:56.480
its reach and field impact. We will meet you there.
